      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, no, no, it’s not like that at all. I know what you’re getting at, but it’s really just this one specific situation. People assume that because I’m so focused on getting to the bottom of this, my whole life must be falling apart or I must be suspicious of everyone around me, but that is completely untrue. \n\nOutside of this entire nightmare with the dispute about my dad’s records, my life is completely normal. I still go to every single one of my phlebotomy shifts—I usually work the early block, and I haven't missed a single day. I clock in, I do my job, I prep my tray, I draw the blood, and I am completely professional with my patients and my coworkers. I don't have any issues with them. Nobody at work is out to get me, and I don't think anyone there is doing anything wrong, except for whoever is touching that specific electronic record. \n\nAnd at home, it's the same thing. I pay my bills on time, my water and electric are never late, I take care of myself... I do my grocery shopping on Sundays, I keep my apartment clean, I make sure my scrubs are washed and ironed for the week. I manage all my normal day-to-day responsibilities without any problem whatsoever. I'm a fully functioning adult! I'm not sitting around paralyzed by paranoia or thinking my landlord or the cashier at the grocery store is part of some grand scheme. It's literally just this one, specific, undeniable issue with the clinic system and my dad's chart. Everything else in my life is totally fine and completely manageable, which is why it’s so insulting when people treat me like I’ve lost my grip on reality just because I won't drop this one very real thing.",
